vkd3d: Requiem clamps the wave size in most places, and the rest is the bug.

**docs/TODO.md #56, and this is the mechanism the entry never had.** Five
compute shaders were dumped from the broken scene and disassembled. Three are
`LocalSize 32 1 1`, and they read `WaveGetLaneCount()` six times: three reads go
through `UMin(32, SubgroupSize)`, and the load-bearing ones do not.

    %402 = OpLoad %uint %SubgroupSize        ; raw
    %403 = OpIMul %uint %402 %401            ; base   = waveSize * groupIdx
    %513 = OpIAdd %uint %509 %138            ; + WavePrefixCountBits(true), 0..31
    %540 = OpIAdd %uint %539 %402            ; stride by waveSize

The shader means wave size to equal the group size. **On this device it is
double that.** `threadsize_base` defaults to 64 (`freedreno_dev_info.py:108`)
and neither `a7xx_base` nor `a8xx_base` overrides it, so Turnip reports
`minSubgroupSize == maxSubgroupSize == 64`. Lane ranks then cover 0..31 of a
window advanced by 64, and `ceil(count / 64)` dispatches half the groups:
**exactly every second item is never processed.**

That is the symptom, precisely. Deterministic, because it is arithmetic. The
objects still exist to the CPU, so collision holds against walls that never
draw. Nothing logs an error, because nothing is wrong -- the shader ran and did
what it was told. And only *some* geometry disappears, because the two
`LocalSize 128` shaders in the same set clamp to 128 and are correct. Desktop
never sees it: NVIDIA reports 32, and RADV picks wave32 for small compute
groups.

vkd3d cannot fix it by asking for a narrower subgroup. The shaders carry no
`WaveSize` metadata, so `state.c` prepends no `requiredSubgroupSize`, and
`32 % 64 != 0` keeps `REQUIRE_FULL_SUBGROUPS` off as well.

**Upstream already fixed this for PRAGMATA, and `pragmata_hashes` becomes
`re_engine_hashes` because it was never per-game.** All five shaders dumped
from Requiem carry the entry point name `PersistentClusterCulling`,
byte-identical to the one PRAGMATA matches, because it is RE Engine's own
culling pass.

**Requiem takes the clamp globally on top of that, and it is a measurement
rather than a preference.** Matching by name fixed the missing world geometry
and left shadows flickering. The dumped sample is five shaders all from that
one entry point, so it says nothing about what Requiem calls its *shadow*
culling pass -- PRAGMATA's second entry is a guess at the name and not a
sighting of it. A name that does not match is a shader that keeps the bug.

Global costs precision and buys certainty. The quirk gates itself on the
workgroup being exactly 32 threads (`dxil_waveops.cpp`), and it rewrites the
`SubgroupSize` *read* rather than any individual use -- so on the three reads
these shaders already clamp by hand the result is `min(32, min(64, 32))`, which
is 32 and unchanged. `min` is idempotent, so the worst it can do to correct
code is add an instruction that folds away. It reaches every 32-thread shader
in the title whatever that shader is called, which is the property the named
match does not have.

**`ENABLE_FAIR_SCHEDULING` stays on the entry points rather than going global,
because it is not a marker the driver ignores.** `dxil_converter.cpp:1142`
emits a `NonSemantic.dxil-spirv.quirks` instruction, `spirv_to_nir.c:1012`
reads it into `occupancy_bounded_workgroup_fairness`, and `ir3_shader.c:607`
turns that into `cs.round_robin_mode` -- **round-robin wave dispatch on the
Adreno.** A persistent-thread loop spins pulling work from a queue, and without
fairness a wave that will not yield can starve the ones behind it. That is why
PRAGMATA pairs it with the clamp, and a8xx sets `round_robin_errata = False` so
the mode is usable. The culling shaders want it; nothing else in the title has
asked for it.

